%T A153431 6009549731752,89984938946056,43000687652274618,5947629516975297508,
%U A153431 740560706756591347900,76993117812161143387438
%N A153431 a(n) is the smallest number m such that all n+1 numbers m*10^k+1 k=0,1, ...,n are prime.
%C A153431 If n<m and r=10^n*a(m)*a(n)-1 is prime then r has at least n+1 representations of the form p*q-(p+q) where p & q are prime.
%C A153431 For n>3, 7 divides a(n). a(10) is greater than 4*10^10.
%H A153431 Jacob Glickman, <a href="https://github.com/jhg023/first-knowns">first-knowns: exhaustive search for A153431</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%H A153431 Carlos Rivera, <a href="https://www.primepuzzles.net/puzzles/puzz_482.htm">Puzzle 482. Two Bergot questions</a>, The Prime Puzzles & Problems Connection.
%e A153431 28+1, 280+1, 2800+1, 28000+1, 280000+1 & 2800000+1 are prime and 28 is the smallest such number so a(5)=28.
%Y A153431 Cf. A153432.
